<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catullus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 84–c. 54 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaius Valerius Catullus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Valerius Catullus was a Roman poet from Verona, active in the late Republic. His surviving collection of 116 poems includes love poems to a woman he called Lesbia, invective against public figures and learned poems modelled on Alexandrian Greek verse. He was the leading figure of the 'new poets' and strongly influenced later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lyric and elegy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catullus.jpg" alt="Catullus" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schorle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catull_Sirmione.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
